<summary>
<span class="ptitle">Deep dive appendix</span>
<span class="deep-dive-title">The Unsettled Frontier</span>
<span class="deep-dive-sub">The most recent, most consequential work in machine reasoning — reported while the ink is still wet and the verdicts are still in dispute.</span>
</summary>
<div class="deep-dive-body">
<p class="lede"><span class="drop">T</span>he first appendix was the director's cut — settled history, scenes that didn't fit but were never in doubt. This one is different, and you should read it with a different posture. Everything here is from 2023 to 2026; much of it is so fresh that the mathematical community has not finished arguing about what it means. Some of it will look, in five years, like the moment the ground shifted. Some will look like a press release that fooled a lot of people for a week. The honest trouble is that, right now, <em>nobody can fully tell which is which</em> — and that is precisely why it belongs in a course about valid inference. This is the hype filter not as a teaching exercise but as a survival skill, applied live, to claims still warm from the lab.</p>
<section>
<p class="sec-eyebrow"><span class="n">Dispatch I</span></p>
<h2>The provers grew up — and went open</h2>
<p>The main page left the AI-proving story at AlphaProof: a closed system, behind a corporate wall, requiring days of computation per problem. Since then the field has done something the larger AI world rarely does — it has gone <em>open</em>, and it has gotten startlingly good. In 2025, anyone with a decent graphics card could download a model that solves olympiad-level mathematics in formal, machine-checked Lean. That is the quiet earthquake.</p>

<p>To follow it you need to understand the scoreboard. Machine provers are ranked on shared <em class="term">benchmarks</em>{% tip 'A benchmark is a standardized test set used to compare systems under stated conditions.' %} — frozen sets of problems everyone tests against. Three matter. <strong>miniF2F</strong> (introduced 2022) is a few hundred olympiad-style problems and was, until recently, the gold standard; it is now nearly <em>saturated</em>, with the best systems scoring near the ceiling. <strong>PutnamBench</strong> (2024) is harder — problems from the legendarily brutal Putnam undergraduate competition, where the median human score is famously <em>zero</em>. And <strong>FrontierMath</strong> (2024) is harder still: research-level problems that take human experts hours or days, designed so that today's models score in the single or low double digits. As one benchmark saturates, the field builds a taller one. The scoreboard is a ladder, and the rungs keep getting added above.</p>

